# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_ring_create(const vx_ring_params *params, vx_ring_handles *out);
vx_status vx_ring_notify(vx_handle end);
int64_t vx_ring_put_handles(vx_handle end, const vx_handle *handles,
                            uint32_t count);
int64_t vx_ring_take_handles(vx_handle end, uint32_t slot, vx_handle *handles,
                             uint32_t capacity);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A ring is the data plane between a client and a server: one VMO both map, holding a submission queue the client fills and the server drains, a completion queue going the other way, and an arena for each side's payloads (see the kernel's design, 01 §4.3).
The kernel makes the VMO and writes its header, and never reads the ring again; the queues are the two sides' business, and `lib/vx-ring` is the protocol they keep.
What the kernel keeps is per end: a doorbell, and a side channel for handles, which an entry in shared memory cannot carry.

`vx_ring_create` makes a ring as <params> asks: `sq_entries` and `cq_entries`, powers of two from 1 to 4096; `sqe_size` and `cqe_size`, multiples of 16 up to 256; and `client_arena` and `server_arena`, in bytes up to 1 GiB each, rounded up to pages.
It writes three handles into <out>: `client` and `server`, the two ends, and `memory`, the VMO.
The VMO's header is what `vx_ring_layout` makes of <params>, and its `size` is the VMO's; each side maps it with `as_map` and checks the header against the parameters its own protocol expects (`vx_ring_attach`), since whoever made the ring may be hostile.
Each end's handle has READ, WRITE, WAIT, SIGNAL, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and INSPECT; the VMO's has READ, WRITE, MAP, DUPLICATE, TRANSFER and INSPECT.
The caller keeps one end and sends the other, with the VMO, to its peer, usually in reply to a channel message.
No handle is needed to make a ring.

`vx_ring_notify` rings the doorbell of the peer of <end>, which needs SIGNAL: it adds one to a counter the peer's end holds.
A producer calls it only when the consumer has said it sleeps (`vx_ring_produce` returns true), so with both sides busy neither enters the kernel.
A consumer about to sleep reads its own doorbell with `counter_read` on its end (READ), announces the sleep, checks the queue once more, and binds its end to a port with `VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE` and a threshold one past what it read (`port_bind`, WAIT on the end); a binding with `VX_TRIGGER_PEER_CLOSED` fires when the peer's end is gone.
`counter_read` and `port_bind` accept a ring end in place of a counter for this.

`vx_ring_put_handles` and `vx_ring_take_handles` make the one call `ring_xfer_handles`, with `VX_RING_PUT` and `VX_RING_TAKE`.
Both need WRITE on <end>.
`vx_ring_put_handles` moves <count> handles, 1 to `VX_RING_SLOT_HANDLES` (4), out of the caller's table into a free slot for the peer, and returns the slot's number, 0 to `VX_RING_SLOTS` - 1 (15); the sender names it in an entry's `handle_slot` with `VX_SQE_HANDLES`.
Each handle needs TRANSFER, and keeps its rights; none may be <end> or its peer, and none may appear twice.
If one is refused, none moves.
`vx_ring_take_handles` takes the handles from the peer's slot <slot> into the caller's table, writes them into <handles>, and returns how many, freeing the slot.
Each direction has its own 16 slots.
Handles left in slots when both ends have gone are closed with the ring.

# EXAMPLES

A consumer's sleep, from the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`):

```c
static void ring_sleep(vx_ring *r, vx_handle end, vx_handle port) {
  int64_t seen = vx_counter_read(end);
  if (vx_ring_prepare_sleep(r)) {
    vx_packet pk;
    vx_port_bind(port, end, VX_TRIGGER_COUNTER_GE, 1, (uint64_t)seen + 1);
    vx_port_wait(port, after_ms(2000), 0, &pk, 1);
  }
  vx_ring_end_sleep(r);
}
```

A handle through the side channel, from the same test:

```c
int64_t slot = vx_ring_put_handles(h.client, &counter, 1);
CHECK(slot >= 0);
CHECK(vx_counter_read(counter) == VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE); // it left our table
```

